## Why This Is Asked
Tests a concrete, real-world scenario: flag evaluation under bursty flash sale traffic; focuses on end-to-end latency, throughput, and proper isolation between evaluation logic and data stores.

## Key Concepts
- Load profiles with burst tolerance testing
- Isolation of compute path from data layer
- End-to-end latency metrics (p95/p99), error rate monitoring
- Performance instrumentation and rapid diagnosis

## Code Example
```javascript
// k6 example: flag evaluation load test
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep, check } from 'k6';

export let options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '5m', target: 1000 },
    { duration: '60m', target: 5000 },
    { duration: '55m', target: 3000 },
    { duration: '5m', target: 0 }
  ],
  thresholds: {
    http_req_duration: ['p(95)<100', 'p(99)<200'],
    http_req_failed: ['rate<0.01']
  }
};

export default function() {
  const headers = {
    'X-User-Key': `user_${Math.random()}`,
    'X-Flag-Set': 'flash-sale-flags'
  };

  const response = http.get('http://localhost:8080/evaluate', { headers });
  check(response, {
    'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200,
    'response time <100ms': (r) => r.timings.duration < 100
  });
  sleep(0.001);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes ability to design an end-to-end test with real-world dependencies (MongoDB, external service) and basic backpressure, plus simple workload modeling. It checks how a candidate isolates bottlenecks across validation, DB writes, and external calls.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end latency measurement
- Workload modeling and pacing
- Bottleneck identification across components
- Basic backpressure strategies and error-rate monitoring

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example k6 script skeleton to simulate signup load
